comments.body:
@gunars Thanks for the bidirectional tests and for distinguishing AP-side association evidence from NetworkManager secret-agent failures. Your 0157-calibration results are useful evidence for retaining this PR's 14,2/14,3 boundary rather than widening it to 13,3.

One limit on generalization: our MacBookPro13,3 / `106b:015a`, using the separate 285753-based calibration in #10141, was connected at **5600 MHz, channel 120 / 80 MHz** on custom `7.2.4-arch1-2-mbp133` on September 15. That was a live SSH/`iw link` observation, not merely channels listed by a scan. The result is recorded in https://github.com/omacom/omarchy/pull/10141#issuecomment-5688899475. Therefore “any 5 GHz AP for these machines has to be on channels 36–48” is broader than the evidence supports.

On September 16, after booting custom `7.2.5-4-omarchy-mbp133`, the same host connected on **2412 MHz** with those calibration files unchanged (SHA-256 `a1a13fe89cf570ce94237cd27de36030584e8ec7084c3447c20c4fd668411dba`); 5 GHz networks were also visible. This is across different boots, not a same-kernel controlled dual-band A/B test, and we did not collect AP-side registration tables.

These observations do not refute your specific machine/file result, isolate `sromrev` here, or establish the CLM behavior across models. We have not tested your 144-key file or its single-line variants and have not changed our working calibration or reloaded the radio for this reply.

@gutterguys1 Thank you for the additional 14,2 report; it supports the existing model gate. Successful 2.4 GHz association with the installed file remains a separate test from stronger 2.4 GHz scan signal.
